{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Ten.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.753,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Wet Paint Bench.",
"start": 2.3529999999999998,
"end": 3.697,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "At half past two on a breezy afternoon, Hedley Truscott finished painting the benches along the harbour wall a bright, cheerful green. He put a sign on each one that said Wet Paint, and told everyone who passed that the paint would stay tacky until at least eight o'clock that evening.",
"start": 5.097,
"end": 22.163,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"Six hours, it says on the tin,\" he told Agnes Bell. \"Mind your skirt.\"",
"start": 23.013,
"end": 27.621,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "On the steps of the lifeboat station, just along from the benches, stood the charity collection box, shaped like a little model lifeboat. It was there at three o'clock, because the coxswain saw it. At four o'clock, it was gone.",
"start": 28.471,
"end": 42.53,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes and Constable Ollie Penrose spoke to the people who had been nearby.",
"start": 43.38,
"end": 48.009,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Hedley himself had green paint all over his hands and the knees of his overalls. But from half past two until well after four, he had been helping Ollie carry crates up the slipway, and Ollie had been with him the whole time.",
"start": 48.859,
"end": 62.598,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Wenna Polglaze had spent the hour from three until four at a window table in the Kettle and Gull, with Agnes refilling her teapot.",
"start": 63.448,
"end": 71.64,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The third person was Mr Kemp, a visitor in a smart cream linen suit.",
"start": 72.49,
"end": 76.757,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I've been sitting on that green bench by the harbour wall, the one nearest the lifeboat station, from three o'clock till four,\" said Mr Kemp. \"Just watching the boats. I didn't see anybody take anything.\"",
"start": 77.607,
"end": 89.959,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes looked at the bench, still shining wet in the sun. Then she looked at Mr Kemp's cream trousers. They were spotless.",
"start": 90.809,
"end": 98.147,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took the collection box?",
"start": 99.247,
"end": 101.893,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 103.093,
"end": 104.117,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 105.017,
"end": 110.243,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 114.743,
"end": 115.767,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Mr Kemp took the collection box.",
"start": 116.867,
"end": 118.915,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "He said he had been sitting on the green bench nearest the lifeboat station for a whole hour. But Hedley had only painted it at half past two, and the paint would stay tacky until eight. Anyone who sat on that bench would have stood up with bright green stripes on the back of their trousers. Mr Kemp's cream trousers didn't have a single mark. He had never sat on that bench at all.",
"start": 119.765,
"end": 140.885,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Hedley was covered in paint, but Ollie had been with him the whole afternoon. Wenna had been in the tea room with Agnes. Mr Kemp's alibi was the only one that could not be true.",
"start": 141.735,
"end": 152.743,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The little lifeboat box was found in the boot of Mr Kemp's car, still full. He said he had thought it was a toy and meant it for his grandson, which nobody quite believed. He put every coin back, and then, under Ollie's steady gaze, added a rather generous note of his own.",
"start": 153.593,
"end": 170.895,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
